  "walking": [
   "This museum began with one doctor's collection. Sir Hans Sloane died in 1753 and left more than seventy-one thousand items to the nation, on one condition: twenty thousand pounds for his heirs. Parliament raised the money with a national lottery, which is one way to fund a museum, and the British Museum Act received royal assent on the seventh of June 1753. Some of Sloane's things are still at work. Scientists in the museum's labs study cocoa that he brought back from Jamaica in the seventeenth century.",
   "The natural history collections grew up inside the British Museum, and even after their own building opened in 1881, they legally stayed part of it for another eighty-two years. The museum got its own trustees in 1963, and was officially renamed the Natural History Museum only in 1992. From 1753 to 1992: two hundred and thirty-nine years to get its own name. It has made up for lost time. In 2025 it had more than seven million visits, the most of any attraction in the UK, ahead of the British Museum. The child has overtaken the parent. And the building it got for its trouble is right in front of you."
  ],
  "tip": "Arriving with a wheelchair or a pushchair? The East Gate, on the corner with Exhibition Road, has a ramp: wide, but steep.",
