  "walking": [
   "In Bloomsbury, the natural history collections had run out of room. They were part of the British Museum, and insects and stuffed animals were kept in hot basement rooms or hidden away in drawers. Richard Owen, who founded this museum, had a radical idea: the public had a right to see every species. His critics said a few well-chosen specimens would do.",
   "Owen's argument was whales. The very fact of the whales being the largest animals upon the Earth, he argued, is that which makes it more imperative to illustrate the fact. He won. Moving the zoology collections, huge whale bones and stuffed mammals included, took three hundred and ninety-four trips by horse and cart, over ninety-seven days.",
   "But they arrived a year after the opening. So when the doors opened in 1881, there was not a single whale skeleton on show, and no gallery big enough to hang one. The founder's whole case was whales, and opening day had none. It took until 2017 for a blue whale to reach the very centre of the hall: a hundred and thirty-six years late. The one he would have wanted is about to be over your head."
  ],
  "tip": "Save your best photograph of her for the top balcony, later on this walk. It is one of the best views of her in the museum.",
