  "walking": [
   "The building you are walking through was nearly someone else's. In 1864 the competition to design it was won by Francis Fowke, the designer of the Royal Albert Hall and of parts of the Victoria and Albert Museum. Fowke died the following year, and the job went to a relatively little-known architect called Alfred Waterhouse: born in Liverpool, trained in Manchester, and a Quaker. He was in his mid-thirties when he was appointed, in February 1866. Then politics froze the plans until 1868, and when they thawed, Waterhouse was allowed to swap Fowke's Renaissance design for German Romanesque, the round arches and towers of the front. Building began in 1873 and took nearly eight years.",
   "Owen shaped it from the inside. Under his guidance, the plan kept geology and fossils apart from living animals and plants, just as the old British Museum had done. So the man who drew the Royal Albert Hall never saw this one, and the little-known Quaker built a cathedral. Owen's other contribution is written on the sign ahead. He coined the word."
  ],
  "tip": "One of the busiest galleries in the museum, with a queue at busy times. The museum says weekday afternoons are its quietest.",
